      <h1 class="mb-4 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">Best Free AI PDF to Excel Converter Tools</h1>
      <p class="mb-8 text-lg text-neutral-400">2026 comparison of free AI-powered PDF to Excel converters with confidence scoring.</p>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-300">
        <p>Converting PDFs to Excel is one of the most common data processing tasks for finance teams, analysts, and administrators. But not all PDF to Excel converters are created equal. Traditional converters rely on basic table detection that fails with complex layouts, while modern AI-powered tools use machine learning to understand document structure.</p>

        <p>In this guide, we compare the best free AI PDF to Excel converter tools available in 2026, with a focus on extraction accuracy, ease of use, and the crucial feature of <strong>per-field confidence scoring</strong>.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-100 pt-4">What Makes a Great PDF to Excel Converter?</h2>
        <p>Before comparing tools, let's define what matters in a PDF to Excel converter:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> How well does it preserve table structure, column types, and data formatting?</li>
          <li><strong>Transparency:</strong> Does it show you confidence scores so you know which fields to trust?</li>
          <li><strong>Format support:</strong> Can it handle scanned PDFs, images, and digital documents?</li>
          <li><strong>Export options:</strong> Does it support Excel (.xlsx), CSV, and other formats?</li>
          <li><strong>Free tier:</strong> How much can you do without paying?</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-100 pt-4">1. PDF2DataAI — Free Tier</h2>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Users who need transparent, confidence-scored extraction with a generous free tier.</p>
        <p>PDF2DataAI's free tier offers 10 full extractions per month with per-field confidence scoring, auto-flagging below 90%, and export to Excel (XLSX) and CSV. The confidence scoring feature is unique — no other free PDF to Excel AI converter shows you exactly how confident it is in each field.</p>
        <p><strong>Free tier:</strong> 10 pages/month, full confidence scoring, no sign-up required.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-100 pt-4">2. SmallPDF</h2>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Quick one-off conversions.</p>
        <p>SmallPDF offers a free PDF to Excel converter with basic table detection. It handles simple tables well but struggles with complex layouts, merged cells, and multi-page documents. The free tier limits you to 2 conversions per day and does not offer confidence scoring.</p>
        <p><strong>Free tier:</strong> 2 conversions/day, no confidence scoring.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-100 pt-4">3. Zamzar</h2>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Simple file format conversion.</p>
        <p>Zamzar converts PDF to Excel (XLS) but uses traditional extraction methods without AI. It works well for straightforward tables but fails with complex documents. Free tier limits file size to 50MB and 2 conversions per day.</p>
        <p><strong>Free tier:</strong> 2 conversions/day, 50MB limit.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-100 pt-4">4. Adobe Acrobat Online</h2>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Users already in the Adobe ecosystem.</p>
        <p>Adobe Acrobat's online PDF to Excel converter offers reliable extraction for standard PDFs. It handles formatting well but does not offer AI-powered confidence scoring. The free tier is limited to a few conversions before requiring a subscription.</p>
        <p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Limited conversions, Adobe account required.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-100 pt-4">5. Google Docs (Workaround)</h2>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Basic table extraction with manual cleanup.</p>
        <p>You can upload a PDF to Google Drive, open it in Google Docs, and download as Excel. This is a free workaround but has very limited accuracy — formatting is often lost, tables may merge incorrectly, and there is no AI-powered data extraction or confidence scoring.</p>
        <p><strong>Free tier:</strong> Unlimited, Google account required.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-100 pt-4">Feature Comparison</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-neutral-800">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead><tr class="border-b border-neutral-800 bg-neutral-900/50"><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium text-neutral-400">Feature</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium text-brand-400">PDF2DataAI</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium text-neutral-500">SmallPDF</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium text-neutral-500">Zamzar</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium text-neutral-500">Adobe</th></tr></thead>
